Z||000000||Fierobruiser||11-04-2001||12:06 AM||||I just encountered a idle problem, does anyone have an idea of its' cause? I cold started my 88 2.5 coupe (air temp 70F) and it idled at the normal 900 rpm. I drove about 1/2 mile and stopped at a traffic light and the idle remained at 2200. I tried lifting on the gas pedal with my foot, since I have encountered this problem with a long since dead Toyota Celica, no help. Since I had to go to work, I continued driving the car and it ran fine and would idle down to almost a stall when slowing but as soon as I put in the clutch to come to a stop it would return to its 2200 idle. After driving for a half hour and traveling about 20 miles it all of a sudden started idling at 900 rpm and for the remainder of my trip and the return trip home it has run normally. Has anyone had this happen before? Is it a sign of something about to fail?<P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||11-04-2001||12:30 AM||||Most likely culprit is a vacuum leak.  You need to read "ogres" vac. leake article.  Click on the link at the very top of this page.  High idles is almost always a vac leak.  I recently found mine. It turned out to be a craked EGR tube.  I used a dental mirror to find the hairling crack.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cid_colada||11-04-2001||11:52 AM||||Last time my car did that the ECM burst into flames for a few seconds, filling the car with smoke(and giving my wife and I a good scare). hope this isn't the case with yours,<BR>knock on wood!<P>Matt<P>Black 84 SE||||reg||11
